From: openFyde
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panthor: accept the 32-bit user MMIO offset from 64-bit
 tasks

A 32-bit UMD maps the LATEST_FLUSH_ID page at
DRM_PANTHOR_USER_MMIO_OFFSET_32BIT (1 << 43), because mmap2's pgoff
cannot reach the 64-bit offset (1 << 56). panthor_mmap() only translates
that offset for tasks flagged TIF_32BIT. An i386 or armhf Mesa run by an
emulator such as FEX or box64 lives inside a 64-bit arm64 process, so
the offset reaches drm_gem_mmap() untranslated and fails with -EINVAL.
Mesa then drops the device ("failed to mmap the LATEST_FLUSH_ID register
(err=22)") and falls back to llvmpipe. This hit 32-bit x86 Steam games
under FEX on fydetab_duo.

Upstream fixes this with DRM_IOCTL_PANTHOR_SET_USER_MMIO_OFFSET. Mesa only
issues it on panthor >= 1.5, and this driver reports 1.0. Translate the
32-bit window for every task instead. This cannot shadow a GEM mapping:
on 64-bit kernels the fake GEM offsets end at
DRM_FILE_PAGE_OFFSET_START + DRM_FILE_PAGE_OFFSET_SIZE, about 1 TiB, well
below 1 << 43. A 64-bit task's offsets in [1 << 43, 1 << 56) used to fail.
Now they map the MMIO page, which is all that window can mean.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panthor/panthor_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panthor/panthor_drv.c
@@ -1357,9 +1357,14 @@
 	 * mmap2's pgoffset field, we need to make the MMIO offset arch
 	 * specific. This converts a user MMIO offset into something the kernel
 	 * driver understands.
+	 *
+	 * Do it for 64-bit tasks too: an emulator (FEX, box64) running a
+	 * 32-bit UMD is a 64-bit task that still uses the 32-bit offset.
+	 * Fake GEM offsets end far below OFFSET_32BIT, so nothing else can
+	 * live in [OFFSET_32BIT, OFFSET_64BIT).
 	 */
-	if (test_tsk_thread_flag(current, TIF_32BIT) &&
-	    offset >= DRM_PANTHOR_USER_MMIO_OFFSET_32BIT) {
+	if (offset >= DRM_PANTHOR_USER_MMIO_OFFSET_32BIT &&
+	    offset < DRM_PANTHOR_USER_MMIO_OFFSET_64BIT) {
 		offset += DRM_PANTHOR_USER_MMIO_OFFSET_64BIT -
 			  DRM_PANTHOR_USER_MMIO_OFFSET_32BIT;
 		vma->vm_pgoff = offset >> PAGE_SHIFT;
